1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,819
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

2
00:00:04,819 --> 00:00:07,228
Every morning OROS proposes.

3
00:00:07,228 --> 00:00:17,812
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

4
00:00:17,812 --> 00:00:21,770
Inside the book today: 5 positions up, 7 down.

5
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:24,179
Best, AAPL at +4.01 percent.

6
00:00:24,179 --> 00:00:25,986
Worst, ANET at -5.83.

7
00:00:25,986 --> 00:00:30,977
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

8
00:00:30,977 --> 00:00:38,032
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -7.78% per trade over 60 graded positions.

9
00:00:38,032 --> 00:00:45,088
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

10
00:00:45,088 --> 00:00:50,251
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

11
00:00:50,251 --> 00:01:02,642
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 267 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

12
00:01:02,601 --> 00:01:09,936
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

13
00:01:09,936 --> 00:01:12,177
Nothing here is financial advice.

14
00:01:12,177 --> 00:01:15,166
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
